# kvm — real-world examples

## Verify Virtualization Technology (VT) is enabled in the BIOS

```sh
grep -E 'svm|vmx' /proc/cpuinfo
# vmx = Intel, svm = AMD — no output means VT is off or unsupported
```

## Xen vs KVM (interview classic)

Xen and KVM are the two main Linux hypervisors. Xen requires booting a special
Xen kernel; KVM is a kernel module, so no extra kernel is needed. KVM does full
virtualization out of the box (with VT-x/AMD-V); classic Xen defaults to
paravirtualization.

## Homelab: install the KVM stack on Tumbleweed

```sh
sudo zypper install -t pattern kvm_server kvm_tools
sudo systemctl enable --now libvirtd
sudo usermod -aG libvirt david    # manage VMs without sudo (re-login)
```

## Homelab: everyday VM management with virsh

```sh
virsh list --all              # all VMs, running or not
virsh start tumbleweed-test
virsh shutdown tumbleweed-test
virsh console tumbleweed-test # serial console (exit: Ctrl+])
```

## Homelab: create a VM from an ISO with virt-install

```sh
virt-install --name tumbleweed-test --memory 4096 --vcpus 2 \
  --disk size=40 --cdrom ~/isos/openSUSE-Tumbleweed-DVD-x86_64.iso \
  --os-variant opensusetumbleweed
```

## Homelab: check KVM is actually loaded

```sh
lsmod | grep kvm       # expect kvm_intel or kvm_amd
```

| Command            | What it does                    |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------- |
| virsh list --all   | list all defined VMs            |
| virsh start/shutdown <vm> | power control            |
| virsh console <vm> | attach to serial console        |
| virt-install       | create a new VM                 |
| lsmod \| grep kvm  | confirm module loaded           |
